From dbff443ced05222d24f660bb9ae0ba24cab1b341 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <assistant@example.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 02:07:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix FSR touch sensor site Z-misalignment and reward-audit
 Potential Decay false positive

- assets/humanoid/humanoid.xml: FSR touch sensor sites were positioned
  3.4cm below the actual foot sole collision geometry (local z=-0.0541
  vs sole box bottom at z=-0.0200), so all 8 FSR touch channels always
  read 0 throughout the entire version_9 training run. This silently
  killed the both_feet_contact reward component (weight=8.0) and fed
  degenerate FSR+ZMP observations to the policy. Fixed by moving sites
  to the sole's bottom face and enlarging their box extents to reliably
  capture the corner-concentrated contact points that MuJoCo's
  box-vs-plane primitive collision generates. X,Y positions are
  unchanged to preserve the RobotConfig.FSR_POSITIONS ABI contract.
  Verified both_feet_contact flips 0.0 -> 1.0 and FSR channels go
  nonzero, in pure MuJoCo and via the real MJX training env/pipeline.

- train/train_mjx.py: _audit_reward_metrics()'s Potential Decay check
  compared raw episode-cumulative potential/pbrs_reward sums, which
  grow simply because episode_alive lengthens over training (131->500
  steps), and flagged pbrs_reward as anomalous whenever it was
  meaningfully negative -- which is the expected steady-state behavior
  of PBRS under gamma=0.99 discounting ((gamma-1)*potential), not a
  sign error in compute_potential(). Normalized both metrics to
  per-step values and compare pbrs_reward/step against its theoretical
  discount-decay expectation instead of an absolute threshold. Verified
  0 alerts on the real version_9 log (down from 151 originally / 17
  after normalization alone), while still firing when pbrs_reward is
  synthetically decoupled from potential.

All 38 existing pytest tests pass with these changes.
